        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          This flight of the Shuttle Columbia was the 27<sup>th</sup> for the orbiter and its first mission since undergoing major modifications after its last flight in 1999.  STS-109, also known as the Hubble Space Telescope Servicing Mission 3B, was the 108<sup>th</sup> flight in the history of the Shuttle program.  The seven-member crew consisted of Commander Scott D. Altman, Pilot Duane G. Carey, Payload Commander John M. Grunsfield, and Mission Specialists Nancy Jane Currie, Richard M. Linnehan, James H. Newman, and Michael J. Massimino.
<P>
The main objective of the 11-day STS-109 mission was to service the Hubble Space Telescope.  Crewmembers rejuvenated the telescope in a series of five spacewalks.  After grasping the telescope and pulling it into the payload bay, the spacewalkers, assisted by Mission Specialist Nancy Jane Currie operating the Shuttle’s robotic arm, installed new and improved equipment that gave the telescope more power, a new module to dispense the power, and a cameral able to see twice as much area, with more speed and clarity.  They also installed an experimental cooling system in hope of restoring life the Near-Infrared Camera and Multi-Object Spectrometer.
<P>
Life sciences experiments on this mission included nine Detailed Supplementary Objective (DSO) experiments.  A DSO is a NASA-sponsored investigation performed by Space Shuttle crewmembers.  These studies are designed to require minimal crew time, power and stowage.  Biomedical DSOs focus on operational concerns, including space motion sickness, cardiovascular deconditioning, muscle loss, changes in coordination and balance strategies, radiation exposure, pharmacokinetics and changes in the body's biochemistry.
<P>
Two Development Test Objective (DTO) experiments were also performed during this mission. A DTO is a NASA-sponsored investigation performed by Space Shuttle crewmembers to evaluate new hardware and procedures involving the Orbiter, its subsystems, and its support equipment.
<P>
After logging about 3.9 statute miles, the Shuttle Columbia landed at Kennedy Space Center in Florida on March 12, 2002.  This landing marked the 58th landing at KSC in the history of the Shuttle program.<END>
        </div>

